      "methods": {
        "addLiquidity(address,uint256,uint256)": {
          "notice": "Single sided liquidity add. More economic at low/moderate liquidity amounts. Mathematically works as adding all tokens and swapping back to 1 token at no fee.         R = (1 + X_supplied/X_initial)^(1/N) - 1         LP_minted = R * LP_total When adding ETH, the inputToken address to be used is the NULL address. A fee is applied to prevent zero fee swapping through liquidity add/remove. Note that this method suffers from two forms of slippage.   1. Slippage from single sided add which is modeled with 15 internal swaps   2. Slippage from the numerical approximation required for calculation. When adding a large amount of liquidity when compared with the existing liquidity for the selected token, the slippage can become quite significant. The smart contract limits the maximum input amount at 100% of the existing liquidity, at which point the slippage is 29.2% (due to 1) + 9.3% (due to 2)"
        },
        "addMultiple(address[],uint256[])": {
          "notice": "Multi-token liquidity add. More economic for large amounts of liquidity. Simply takes in all 16 listed tokens in ratio and mints the LPs accordingly. For ETH, the inputToken address to be used is the NULL address. A fee is applied to prevent zero fee swapping through liquidity add/remove."
        },
        "bootstrapNewToken(address,uint256,address)": {
          "notice": "When a token is delisted and another one gets listed in its place, the users can call this function to provide liquidity for the new token in exchange for the old token. The ratio should be set such that the users have a financial incentive to perform this transaction."
        },
        "bootstrapNewTokenWithBonus(address,uint256,address,address)": {
          "notice": "Emergency bonus withdrawal when bootstrapping is expected to remain incomplete A fraction is specified (for example 5%) that is then rewarded in bonus tokens on top of the regular bootstrapping output tokens."
        },
        "changeListing(address,address,uint112)": {
          "notice": "Initiates process to delist one token and list another."
        },
        "constructor": {
          "notice": "Sets up default configuration Initialize with ordered list of 15 token addresses (ETH is always listed) Doesn't do any checks. Make sure you ONLY add well behaved ERC20s!!"
        },
        "lockExchange()": {
          "notice": "Sets exchange lock, under which swap and liquidity add (but not remove) are disabled"
        },
        "removeLiquidity(uint256,address,uint256)": {
          "notice": "Single sided liquidity withdrawal. More efficient at lower liquidity amounts. Mathematically withdraws 16 tokens in ratio and then swaps 15 back in at no fees. Calculates the following:        R = LP_burnt / LP_initial        X_out = X_initial * (1 - (1 - R)^N) No fee is applied for withdrawals. For ETH output, use the NULL address as outputToken."
        },
        "removeMultiple(uint256,address[])": {
          "notice": "Multi-token liquidity removal. More economic for large amounts of liquidity. Returns all 16 listed tokens in ratio and burns the LPs accordingly."
        },
        "setAdmin(address)": {
          "notice": "Sets admin address for emergency exchange locking"
        },
        "setDeListingBonus(uint64)": {
          "notice": "Sets delisting bonus as emergency measure to complete a (de)listing when it gets stuck."
        },
        "setTradingFee(uint64)": {
          "notice": "Sets trading fee (actually calculates using 1-fee) as a 0.64 fixed point number."
        },
        "swap(address,address,uint256,uint256)": {
          "notice": "Allows users to swap between any two tokens listed on the DEX. Follows the x*y=k swap invariant hyperbole For ETH trades, send the ETH with the transaction and use the NULL address as inputToken."
        },
        "unlockExchange()": {
          "notice": "Resets exchange lock."
        }
      },
      "notice": "Trades between two tokens follow the local bonding curve x*y=k The number of tokens used is hard coded to 16 for efficiency reasons.",
      "version": 1
    }
  },
